  "sources": "RBI ATM, POS and card statistics",
  "post": "September's RBI card data: the government banks are taking the rupees.\n\n1. We keep swiping. The rupees are not keeping up.\n\nCredit card swipes at shops are up 23.4% in a year. The rupee value is up only 7.2%. In September alone, value growth slowed from 9.1% to 7.2%, while swipes barely moved (23.5% to 23.4%).\n\n2. Public sector banks took most of the growth.\n\nThey have 22.6% of card value at shops, but took 71.0% of its growth. A month ago that was 51.0%. Private banks hold 72.7% and grew just 1.4%.\n\n3. SBI up, HDFC down.\n\nSBI grew 25.2%. HDFC Bank, still the biggest at 26.5%, fell 6.7%, after growing 0.9% in August. Part of that is a high base: HDFC was growing 36.1% a year ago.\n\n4. Small finance banks are adding cards fast.\n\nOnly 2.1% of all cards, but 12.3% of the new ones, up 117.4% to 26.94 lakh.\n\nAnd the question I can't answer yet: private bank swipes are up 21.6% with value up 1.4%. Public bank swipes are up 13.6% with value up 26.6%. Why are the two moving so differently?\n\nData: RBI ATM, POS and card statistics, Sep 2026 · indiacreditlens.com",
